{-
   The message digests - `Digest`, and Analysis-Library.md §3.2.

   **Known answers and one property.** Every digest here is checked against values produced by
   something else - `sha256sum`, `openssl`, `zlib.crc32`, and the reference implementations of
   Murmur3-32 and xxHash32 - at five lengths chosen for where they fall against a sixty-four-byte
   block: nothing, less than a block, one that pads into a second block, one in between, and 768
   bytes so that the bulk loop runs twelve times. A digest that got the padding wrong passes the
   short cases and fails the second, which is why the second is there.

   The property is that **streaming and one-shot agree**: a digest fed one byte at a time has to
   answer what the same bytes hashed in one call answer, at every length. That is what exercises the
   buffering in `absorb` - the top-up path, the "did not fill" early return, and the tail - and it is
   the half of this module a known-answer test cannot reach, since every published vector is a
   one-shot.

   Whirlpool is not here: it is 64-bit arithmetic throughout and therefore native-only, so it has a
   fixture of its own without the `.js` marker. `DigestSha.yana` beside this one is this file
   compiled for a target that claims the SHA extension, which is what tests the other of the two
   compression functions the file selector chooses between - see `Digest/Hardware.sha.yana`.
-}

import Native
import Test

{-
   One `@noinline` helper per algorithm, and the split is about compile time rather than style.

   Inlined into a single body, the forty checks below are **8,929 lines of IR** - a third of the
   whole program, where nothing else in the corpus exceeds a thousand - and the two passes that
   promote storage over an inlined body are close to quartic in the size of one function. That made
   this fixture 1.68s of the library corpus's 7.3s, and `DigestSha.yana` beside it another 1.68s.
   See test/bench/findings.md §63, which has the measurement and the passes.

   Eight bodies of five checks cost a fraction of one body of forty, and `@noinline` is what keeps
   them apart afterwards - without it the inliner puts the same body back together and nothing is
   saved. **Nothing about what is asserted depends on the shape**: the vectors are built once and
   handed over as the `Flat(U8)` every entry point in `Digest` already takes, and each check keeps a
   line of its own, so a failure still names both the algorithm and the length. Undo it when the
   passes are fixed.
-}

@noinline fn md5Answers(empty: [U8], abc: [U8], twoBlocks: [U8], pangram: [U8],
                        long: [U8]) -> {}:
    checkEqual(hexOf(md5Of(empty)), "d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e")
    checkEqual(hexOf(md5Of(abc)), "900150983cd24fb0d6963f7d28e17f72")
    checkEqual(hexOf(md5Of(twoBlocks)), "8215ef0796a20bcaaae116d3876c664a")
    checkEqual(hexOf(md5Of(pangram)), "9e107d9d372bb6826bd81d3542a419d6")
    checkEqual(hexOf(md5Of(long)), "e6899eaaf06fd702f3ed3f988eb19362")

